Let the boot logo scroll away instead of holding the top of the screen.

ps2con_setup() makes room for the logo by setting conp->vc_top = logo_lines,
which moves the *top of the scrolling region* below it.  Those rows are then
excluded from every scroll, so the logo does not move: it holds the top five
rows of a 640x512 PAL console -- about 15% of the screen -- for the whole
session, and long output such as `ls` loses its first lines off the top of a
region that is shorter than the screen.

Stock 2.4 undoes the reservation in exactly three places, all inherited
unchanged by ps2con.c from fbcon.c: a full-screen clear (ps2con_clear), a VT
switch (ps2con_switch), and manual scrollback (ps2con_scrolldelta).  A PS2
Linux boot to a single console hits none of them, so the reservation is
permanent -- this is stock behaviour that simply never showed on a desktop,
where switching to tty2 or a login program clearing the screen releases it
within seconds of boot.

Release it on the first scroll instead, using the same three lines the switch
path already uses.  The logo is drawn into the framebuffer like any other
pixels, so once its rows rejoin the scrolling region it scrolls up and off with
the text below it, which is the behaviour people expect from a boot logo.

Nothing else changes: the logo is still drawn, still occupies the top of the
screen for as long as nothing has scrolled, and a console that never scrolls
keeps it indefinitely.

--- a/drivers/video/ps2con.c
+++ b/drivers/video/ps2con.c
@@ -975,6 +975,16 @@
     if (!count || vt_cons[unit]->vc_mode != KD_TEXT)
 	return 0;

+    /* Hand the logo rows back to the scrolling region on the first scroll,
+       so the logo scrolls up and off with the text that follows it. */
+    if (logo_shown >= 0) {
+	struct vc_data *conp2 = vc_cons[logo_shown].d;
+
+	if (conp2->vc_top == logo_lines && conp2->vc_bottom == conp2->vc_rows)
+	    conp2->vc_top = 0;
+	logo_shown = -1;
+    }
+
     ps2con_cursor(conp, CM_ERASE);

     switch (dir) {
